# Lesson 5, Sample A. Mike's Exercise 4, completed with East-West cards.
title: Your Exercise 4, completed
id: L05-A
type: exercise
lesson: 5
concepts: 5.3
student: S
board: 48
dealer: W
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  A76    KQ7    AJ943  J4
  E  84     84     652    AQ9872
  S  K532   AJ652  K7     T5
  W  QJT9   T93    QT8    K63

auction:
  W Pass
  N 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
  E Pass
  S 2C     = Stayman: do you have a 4-card major?
  W Pass
  N 2D     = No 4-card major
  E Pass
  S 3H*    = Five hearts, game values: you choose
     key: Five hearts and game values: partner chooses between 4♥ and 3NT.
     not 3NT: You have five hearts, and partner may have three. Let partner choose.
     not 2H: That shows 8-9 points; with 11, make a bid partner can't pass.
     not 4H: Partner may have only two hearts. 3♥ asks; 4♥ guesses.
  W Pass
  N 4H     = I have three hearts
     key: Three hearts, so North chooses 4♥.
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  Your 2♣ found no 4-card major in partner's hand, but you have five hearts.
  3♥ says "five hearts, game values; you choose." With three hearts North
  picks 4♥. South is declarer, because South bid hearts first, and so West
  will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 5
  remind: What is the contract?
  remind: Did the opponents bid?

play:
  1   W SQ  N S6  E S8  S SK*
      SK key: Win in hand, and save dummy's ace.
      SK not S2: Did you think about winning the first trick? Ducking lets the defense keep leading spades.
      note: West leads the ♠Q. Win in hand. Dummy's ♠A is saved as a way back to dummy later.
  2   S H2  W H3  N HK  E H4
      note: Draw trumps.
  3   N HQ  E H8  S H5  W H9
      note: Keep drawing trumps.
  4   N H7  E C2  S HA  W HT
      note: All the opponents' trumps are gone.
  5   S DK*  W D8  N D3  E D2
      DK key: Now set up dummy's diamonds.
      DK not CT: Did you look at dummy's long diamonds? They're where your extra tricks come from.
      note: Now set up dummy's diamonds.
      ok: S
  6   S D7  W DT  N DA  E D5
      note: Keep going with diamonds.
  7   N D4  E D6  S H6  W DQ
      note: Ruff the third diamond. Diamonds split 3-3, so dummy's ♦J and ♦9 are now winners.
  8   S S2  W ST  N SA  E S4
      note: Back to dummy with the saved ♠A.
  9   N DJ  E C7  S S3  W S9
      note: Throw a losing spade from hand.
  10  N D9  E C8  S S5  W C3
      note: And the other one.
  11  N C4  E CA  S C5  W C6
      note: The two club losers can't be avoided.
  12  E CQ  S CT  W CK  N CJ
  13  W SJ  N S7  E C9  S HJ
      note: Ruff West's last spade. 11 tricks: 4♥ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: draw trumps, then set up dummy's diamonds, saving dummy's ♠A as
  the way back to them.

  Compare 3NT, played by North. East leads a club and the defense takes the
  first six tricks: ♣K, then ♣Q and four more clubs. In 4♥, the trumps stop
  the clubs after two rounds. This is the point of your 3♥ bid: it gave
  partner the chance to choose the safer game.

  A note on the play: setting up dummy's diamonds this way worked because
  they split 3-3. Against a 4-2 split it would fail, and stronger lines exist,
  such as keeping dummy's ♥Q as a way back to ruff a second diamond. Playing
  for a spade ruff in dummy is about as good. Several lines are close here;
  the bidding is the lesson.
